    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-muslim.jpg" alt="Abu Muslim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Abu_Muslim_(d._755)_from_the_genealogy_(silsilan%C4%81ma),_Cream_of_Histories_(Z%C3%BCbdet-%C3%BCt_Tevarih,_1598).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1598 author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Abbasid Caliphate was founded in 750 CE following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a>, which overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad dynasty</a> at the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-great-zab">Battle of the Great Zab</a> in January of that year. The revolution was driven by widespread discontent with Umayyad Arab exclusivism, a policy that marginalised non-Arab Muslims, particularly Persian converts known as <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a></em>, and was catalysed by the charismatic generalship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a>, a commander of obscure but probably Persian origin operating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-abbas-al-saffah">Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah</a> (r. 750–754), inherited the vast Umayyad territorial empire but faced immediate challenges: Shi'a supporters who had expected a dynasty descended from Ali felt betrayed, the Umayyad survivor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a> escaped to establish a rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, and Abu Muslim himself remained a dangerous independent power. It was the second caliph, al-Mansur (r. 754–775), who served as the dynasty's true architect. He had Abu Muslim executed in 755, eliminating the most dangerous rival power centre, and in 762 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-city-of-baghdad">Round City of Baghdad</a>, Madinat al-Salam or "City of Peace", on the western bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> not far from the ruins of the Sasanian capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, deliberately connecting Abbasid authority to Persian imperial prestige. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> grew within decades into one of the largest cities in the world, and the Abbasids distinguished themselves from their predecessors by embracing Muslims of every ethnicity, welcoming Persian <em>mawali</em> into the bureaucracy, and presenting themselves as the legitimate inheritors of universal Islamic rule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/baghdad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baghdad.jpg" alt="Baghdad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5628442718_b10fc2c47f_o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">USACE HQ , JIM GORDAN, CIV, USACE, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Abbasid Caliphate was one of the most economically sophisticated polities of the medieval world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> sat at the intersection of transcontinental trade routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, making it the commercial hub of the known world at its peak. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s revenues derived from agricultural taxation across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>'s highly productive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawad">Sawad</a>, customs duties on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a> passing through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and remittances from provincial governors across an empire stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The embrace of Persian administrative traditions brought with it a highly developed system of taxation, record-keeping and land assessment. Socially, the Abbasid state was far more cosmopolitan than its predecessor: Persian, Arab and Turkic elements coexisted within the court and administration, while a flourishing urban class of merchants, scholars and artisans gave Baghdad extraordinary cultural and intellectual vitality. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakid family</a>, Persian administrators who served the early caliphs and rose to the height of their power as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> until their sudden fall in 803, exemplified this multicultural elite. Agricultural productivity in Mesopotamia remained high through the early period, sustained by a complex canal and irrigation network that had made the region fertile for millennia. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a> (869–883), a massive revolt of enslaved labourers in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, devastated the agricultural heartland and contributed materially to the caliphate's later economic decline.</p></div><p class="profile-ladder"><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">The social ladder of the Abbasid Caliphate &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid.jpg" alt="Harun al-Rashid" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_Harun_al-Rashid,_AH_170-193.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">8/9th-century master(s), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Abbasid military underwent a fundamental transformation over the dynasty's lifetime. In the early period, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> relied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasani</a> armies that had won the revolution, a mixed Arab and Persian force loyal to the dynasty. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> (r. 786–809), these forces were powerful enough to defeat the Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Emperor Nikephoros</a> I in 806, imposing humiliating tribute terms. The fateful turning point came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> (r. 833–842), who built up a professional slave army of Turkic soldiers, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilman">ghilman</a></em>, recruited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, intending to give the caliph a loyal personal force independent of Arab and Khurasani factions, and moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in 836 to house it. This innovation, militarily effective in the short term, proved institutionally catastrophic: the Turkic commanders quickly learned to control the caliphate by murdering any caliph who resisted their demands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-anarchy">The Anarchy</a> at Samarra (861–870) began with the assassination of al-Mutawakkil and saw four caliphs killed by the very army meant to protect them. Military power increasingly devolved to provincial commanders and then to outright successor dynasties: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> in the east, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> at the centre. The revival under al-Nasir (r. 1180–1225) partially rebuilt caliphal military capacity, enough to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and its hinterland, but the caliphate was never again a first-rank military power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mamun.jpg" alt="al-Ma'mun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Abbasid_Caliph_al-Ma%27mun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Abbasid administrative system represented a significant advance over the Umayyad model. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mansur-abbasid-caliph">Al-Mansur</a> centralised judicial administration, appointed loyal governors across the empire, and laid the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizierate</a>, a chief ministry drawn typically from the Persian bureaucratic tradition, as the primary instrument of imperial government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barmakids">Barmakid family</a> served as viziers and secretaries under several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, building a sophisticated chancery that managed correspondence, taxation and provincial oversight. The legal system was organised around the developing schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a> (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhab">madhhabs</a></em>), with the caliph claiming authority not only as temporal ruler but as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a>, the supreme religious and political sovereign. This dual legitimacy was central to Abbasid ideology, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> (r. 813–833) pushed it furthest by adopting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutazilism">Mu'tazilism</a> as state doctrine and launching the <em>mihna</em>, an inquisition asserting the caliph's right to define theological orthodoxy. The structural weakness of the system was its dependence on personal caliphal authority: once Turkic military commanders began placing and removing caliphs at will after 861, the bureaucracy continued to function but the political coherence it depended on was hollow. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid</a> domination (945–1055), the caliphs retained religious prestige but exercised little or no political authority, serving in effect as a sanctifying institution for the rulers who held actual power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala.jpg" alt="Al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar fī ḥisāb al-jabr wal-muqābala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala">Al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar fī ḥisāb al-jabr wal-muqābala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Image-Al-Kit%C4%81b_al-mu%E1%B8%ABta%E1%B9%A3ar_f%C4%AB_%E1%B8%A5is%C4%81b_al-%C4%9Fabr_wa-l-muq%C4%81bala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Muḥammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Abbasid Caliphate's global impact was disproportionate even to its enormous territorial scope, chiefly because of its role in generating, preserving and transmitting knowledge. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a> (<em>Bayt al-Hikma</em>), traditionally associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> and reaching its zenith under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, sponsored the systematic translation of Greek, Persian, Indian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> texts into Arabic, a project that preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-ptolemy">Claudius Ptolemy</a> for the medieval world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">Al-Khwarizmi</a>'s treatise on calculation by completion and balancing (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala">al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar</a> fī ḥisāb al-jabr wal-muqābala</em>, c. 820), the work that gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> its name, emerged from this environment and permanently transformed mathematics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s trade and diplomatic networks reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, the East African coast and the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, making Arabic a language of international commerce and scholarship. The Abbasid model of multi-ethnic Islamic government, embracing all Muslims under a single religious-political authority, became the template against which later Islamic states measured themselves. Even after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258">Mongol destruction of Baghdad</a> in 1258, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-shadow-caliphate">shadow caliphate</a> maintained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluks</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> (1261–1517) served as an important legitimising institution for Muslim rulers across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. The intellectual output of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Abbasid Golden Age</a>, recovered through Arabic translations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, helped lay the foundations of later European science.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Inventions and discoveries credited here</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/inventions?focus=bimaristan">Hospital (bimaristan)<span>805</span></a><a href="/inventions?focus=algebra">Algebra<span>825</span></a><a href="/inventions?focus=kerosene">Oil refining<span>9th century</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion.jpg" alt="Zanj Rebellion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zanj_Rebellion.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ro4444, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Abbasid Caliphate's decline was the product of a structural contradiction embedded in its own founding success. By opening the empire to Muslims of all ethnicities, the Abbasids enabled the rise of Persian, Turkic and other non-Arab power centres that eventually eclipsed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> themselves. The Turkic <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilman">ghilman</a></em> army built up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> began the military subjugation of the caliphate; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchy-at-samarra">Anarchy at Samarra</a> (861–870) made it irreversible. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj-rebellion">Zanj Rebellion</a> (869–883) wrecked the agricultural economy of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid</a> takeover of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 945 eliminated the last vestiges of caliphal political authority, reducing the institution to a religious symbol, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> who replaced the Buyids in 1055 maintained the same arrangement. A partial revival under al-Nasir (r. 1180–1225) raised brief hopes of genuine restoration, but the Mongol invasion ended the Baghdad caliphate entirely: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a>'s forces took the city in February 1258, executed Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a>, and sacked its libraries, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-shadow-caliphate">shadow caliphate</a> continued in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> patronage until 1517, when the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-conquest-of-egypt">Selim I's conquest of Egypt</a> brought even that ceremonial institution to an end.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Abbasid Caliphate endured in one form or another for 767 years, a testament to the remarkable resilience of its religious legitimacy even as its political substance was repeatedly hollowed out. Its fall marked the definitive end of a unified caliphal authority and the permanent fragmentation of the caliphal institution. It survives in historical memory as the civilisation that gave the medieval world <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, advances in astronomy and medicine, and the preservation of classical learning, an intellectual legacy that outlasted by centuries the political empire that produced it.</em></p>

